WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.057 --> 00:00:03.187
- "Cells at Work!" season one
told us how the body works.

00:00:03.187 --> 00:00:06.440
"Cells at Work!" season
two CODE BLACK tells us

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.510
what we do to our bodies and
how we hurt them, peewoop!

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:13.800
Episode 1: Smoking, Bacteria,
and the Beginning of the End.

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.200
I hope it's not the beginning of the end.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.117
(speaking in foreign language)

00:00:30.493 --> 00:00:32.240
(student yawns)

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:34.900
This is so funny because not
only is this very accurate

00:00:34.900 --> 00:00:37.600
in that an immature red
blood cells and erythroblasts

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:39.210
and now that they become red blood cells,

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:40.230
they are fully matured.

00:00:40.230 --> 00:00:41.950
They are also presenting this

00:00:41.950 --> 00:00:44.300
like a true med school
situation where some people

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:46.510
in the classroom are
sleepy and yawning, me.

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:49.020
Some people are taking notes
and being very aggressive.

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:50.300
What do we know about red blood cells?

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:53.430
Main job is to carry
oxygen throughout the body

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.116
and deliver it to its vital tissues:

00:00:55.116 --> 00:00:58.550
brain, the lungs, the heart, kidneys.

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:00.760
These are vital organs that keep us going.

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:02.220
In fact, if blood vessels,

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:05.050
the little tunnels that red
blood cells travel through,

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.430
are ever blocked, that's
when you have a problem.

00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:09.070
If one of those arteries

00:01:09.070 --> 00:01:10.890
or blood vessels starts to
get blocked in the heart,

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:11.723
you have a heart attack.

00:01:11.723 --> 00:01:14.310
One of them gets blocked in
the brain, you have a stroke.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.800
It's all about maintaining good plumbing

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.120
and allowing these red
blood cells to do their job.

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.439
So I hope they're paying attention.

00:01:20.439 --> 00:01:24.439
(speaking in foreign language)

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:28.510
(laughs) Oxygen/O2.

00:01:30.154 --> 00:01:34.154
(speaking in foreign language)

00:01:38.378 --> 00:01:39.211
Aw.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:46.210
Red blood cells, unfortunately,

00:01:46.210 --> 00:01:48.030
all they do is circulate
throughout the body

00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:50.720
no matter what time of
day it is, day/night.

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:52.790
When a person's sleeping,
you're still circulating blood.

00:01:52.790 --> 00:01:53.740
You got to feed the brain,

00:01:53.740 --> 00:01:56.190
you got to recover the
muscles, all that good stuff.

00:01:56.190 --> 00:01:57.023
Now that being said,

00:01:57.023 --> 00:02:00.690
red blood cells are only
around approximately 120 days.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:01.800
And throughout that time,

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.300
they're constantly getting refreshed

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:04.401
and new ones are being made.

00:02:04.401 --> 00:02:08.401
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:12.760
Basically when the aortic
valve opens, the heart beats

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:15.860
and all the red blood cells
starts shooting out to the body.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:18.090
Whether they go to the top
of the body in the brain

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.550
or to the bottom of the body

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:21.320
to the kidneys and legs, et cetera.

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:24.350
The goal is to get as much oxygen quickly

00:02:24.350 --> 00:02:26.550
to that part of the body

00:02:26.550 --> 00:02:28.680
and then to bring back the red blood cells

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:30.310
that are now deoxygenated

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:32.090
and need to be refreshed by the lungs

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:33.910
back through the venous system.

00:02:33.910 --> 00:02:35.010
Now I know a lot of times

00:02:35.010 --> 00:02:37.510
in textbooks we draw the
arterial system is red

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:38.910
and the venous system is blue.

00:02:38.910 --> 00:02:40.290
If you cut someone's veins,

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.120
I promise the blood is still gonna be red.

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:44.170
It's just simply about
the tint of the blood

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:46.230
when it's below your skin, but in reality,

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:47.984
it is still red, I promise.

00:02:47.984 --> 00:02:49.665
(speaking in foreign language)

00:02:49.665 --> 00:02:52.498
(dramatic music)

00:02:58.300 --> 00:02:59.133
Platelets.

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:13.940
Oh, does this person have an ulcer?

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:16.900
An ulcer is an opening inside the stomach

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:20.190
from the gastric acid
or perhaps an infection

00:03:20.190 --> 00:03:21.814
and as a result, bleeding can occur.

00:03:21.814 --> 00:03:23.542
(speaking in foreign language)

00:03:23.542 --> 00:03:26.292
(magma bubbling)

00:03:33.301 --> 00:03:34.460
I would make a small correction there.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:36.120
It's not the first stage of digestion

00:03:36.120 --> 00:03:38.430
because the first stage of digestion

00:03:38.430 --> 00:03:41.000
is a funny word known as mastication.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:43.510
Mastication is when you chew,

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:45.300
that is the chemical breakdown of food,

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:46.980
that is where digestion begins.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:50.680
In fact, you have salivary
amylase which is an enzyme

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:53.330
that starts breaking down
sugars inside the food

00:03:53.330 --> 00:03:55.410
that you're eating
immediately in your mouth.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:03:56.670
In your mouth. That's right.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.390
So digestion is happening here first.

00:03:58.390 --> 00:04:00.301
That's the first step of
digestion not in the stomach.

00:04:00.301 --> 00:04:01.970
(speaking in foreign language)

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:03.323
Oh, wow. Angry stomach.

00:04:04.351 --> 00:04:07.434
(suspenseful music)

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:26.470
Can you imagine if every time

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:28.730
you didn't take care of your body,

00:04:28.730 --> 00:04:33.100
you underslept, you
overworked, overate, underate,

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:36.910
ate something unhealthy,
overdrank, smoked a cigarette,

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:38.730
did something wrong in general

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:41.410
that your body's cells
actually went on strike

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:42.310
and got mad at you?

00:04:42.310 --> 00:04:45.420
Imagine your stomach was like, "I'm mad.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:47.559
Stop working.

00:04:47.559 --> 00:04:48.707
Agh.

00:04:48.707 --> 00:04:51.880
(pipe explodes)
(both gasp)

00:04:51.880 --> 00:04:52.713
Oh no!

00:04:54.673 --> 00:04:57.256
(man coughing)

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:01.612
Oh no!

00:05:01.612 --> 00:05:04.112
(man gasping)

00:05:05.662 --> 00:05:09.694
(speaking in foreign language)

00:05:09.694 --> 00:05:11.775
Oh no, carbon monoxide poisoning.

00:05:11.775 --> 00:05:14.775
(suspenseful music)

00:05:36.876 --> 00:05:38.060
This is a very accurate description

00:05:38.060 --> 00:05:40.690
of how carbon monoxide
poisoning actually happens.

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:42.400
Because it has a higher affinity,

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.730
meaning it's more likely to
bind with this red blood cell,

00:05:44.730 --> 00:05:47.310
it actually blocks the
ability to carry oxygen.

00:05:47.310 --> 00:05:49.250
So what happens if you have a slow leak

00:05:49.250 --> 00:05:51.260
of carbon monoxide into a small area,

00:05:51.260 --> 00:05:54.640
like a garage or a home, you
actually start getting sleepy,

00:05:54.640 --> 00:05:57.410
you get a little nauseous, a
headache starts developing,

00:05:57.410 --> 00:05:58.650
and what's really dangerous is

00:05:58.650 --> 00:06:00.830
if you do end up falling
asleep, you could die.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:02.550
Also, knowing that the treatment

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:04.040
for carbon monoxide poisoning,

00:06:04.040 --> 00:06:05.350
if you get to the hospital,

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:07.760
is getting a full blast
of O2 in your face.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:08.593
Why?

00:06:08.593 --> 00:06:10.420
Because if you overload the amount

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:11.310
of oxygen you're getting,

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:14.210
hopefully that will
create enough competition

00:06:14.210 --> 00:06:15.830
that your red blood
cells will actually get

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:18.400
some of this oxygen as opposed
to the carbon monoxide.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:19.246
Does that make sense?

00:06:19.246 --> 00:06:20.527
(foot thuds)

00:06:20.527 --> 00:06:24.444
(speaking in foreign language)

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:29.587
Oh pneumococci!

00:06:29.587 --> 00:06:32.587
(bacteria growling)

00:06:41.309 --> 00:06:44.000
So this is why whenever
a patient is diagnosed

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:45.980
with bacterial pneumonia,
one of the first things we do

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:49.870
is institute antibacterial
treatment with antibiotics.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:52.043
The invention of antibiotics
has really gone a long way

00:06:52.043 --> 00:06:55.070
in extending life and
preventing unnecessary death.

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:56.980
However, now we're kind of in this state

00:06:56.980 --> 00:06:58.960
where we're overusing antibiotics

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:01.470
and are not only killing healthy bacteria

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:04.230
in our bodies by mistake
that we need to live,

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:06.610
but also you can create resistance,

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:08.440
where if you don't take antibiotics

00:07:08.440 --> 00:07:09.720
for a long enough period of time,

00:07:09.720 --> 00:07:11.000
or let's say you take them

00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:13.260
and you eradicate 99% of the bacteria,

00:07:13.260 --> 00:07:16.200
you leave the 1% that are
genetically resistant to it.

00:07:16.200 --> 00:07:17.770
And as a result, when they reproduce,

00:07:17.770 --> 00:07:19.320
each time you take the antibiotics,

00:07:19.320 --> 00:07:22.620
your body bacteria actually
start building up a resistance

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:23.620
to those antibiotics.

00:07:23.620 --> 00:07:25.410
Why does that become
problematic? Well, guess what?

00:07:25.410 --> 00:07:26.904
A patient comes in with a pneumonia,

00:07:26.904 --> 00:07:29.800
let's say a sepsis where the
bacteria is now in the blood,

00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:32.310
and then you realize for
all the antibiotic options

00:07:32.310 --> 00:07:34.370
that you have, which we
don't have millions of,

00:07:34.370 --> 00:07:37.490
you actually have no choices
on what to give that patient

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:39.130
because all the bacteria is resistant

00:07:39.130 --> 00:07:40.610
and you've created a superbug.

00:07:40.610 --> 00:07:41.670
This thing looks like a superbug

00:07:41.670 --> 00:07:44.351
but I hope antibiotics will
still work in this case.

00:07:44.351 --> 00:07:46.232
(dramatic music)

00:07:46.232 --> 00:07:47.537
(speaking in foreign language)

00:07:47.537 --> 00:07:49.040
(metal clangs)

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:51.283
(bodies thud)

00:07:51.283 --> 00:07:55.200
(speaking in foreign language)

00:07:58.875 --> 00:07:59.708
Oh!

00:08:00.695 --> 00:08:03.778
That white blood cell got some moves.

00:08:05.130 --> 00:08:07.010
Reminds of Michonne from "Walking Dead."

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:09.125
Who's a "Walking Dead" fan here?

00:08:09.125 --> 00:08:13.125
(speaking in foreign language)

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:17.207
It's funny that they're
going to the carotid artery,

00:08:17.207 --> 00:08:19.360
there was some plaque there visible.

00:08:19.360 --> 00:08:20.972
This does happen, and in fact,

00:08:20.972 --> 00:08:23.990
one of the causes or
potential causes of strokes is

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:27.550
when you have such a bad
occlusion of the carotid arteries,

00:08:27.550 --> 00:08:29.960
you actually impede
blood flow to that area.

00:08:29.960 --> 00:08:32.990
So imagine if you have a lot
of plaque, it's blocked off,

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:35.660
you can't get blood to the
brain, boom, stroke happens.

00:08:35.660 --> 00:08:37.800
Now you do have two carotid arteries

00:08:37.800 --> 00:08:39.080
which makes it a little bit better.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:41.310
So you wanna keep your
carotid arteries as patent

00:08:41.310 --> 00:08:42.870
and as open as possible,
and how do you do that?

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:44.050
Living a healthy lifestyle,

00:08:44.050 --> 00:08:45.310
going for preventive screenings,

00:08:45.310 --> 00:08:46.900
making sure your cholesterol
is under control,

00:08:46.900 --> 00:08:48.900
and know that just because
your cholesterol is high

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:50.400
or the LDL cholesterol,

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:52.030
the bad cholesterol is
high, it doesn't mean

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.840
that you might necessarily
be doing something wrong

00:08:53.840 --> 00:08:57.560
because there are cases where
genetics play a huge role

00:08:57.560 --> 00:08:59.970
so much so that your
cholesterol is so high

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:02.430
that you could never even get
it that high with a bad diet

00:09:02.430 --> 00:09:03.800
as a result of your genetics.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:06.830
This is known as familial
hypercholesterolemia.

00:09:06.830 --> 00:09:08.470
There are specific medications

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:12.220
that are recently been released
called PCSK9 inhibitors.

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:13.930
Again, we don't like
jumping to medications

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:15.620
but if you have such a dangerous LDL

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:17.000
that it's causing you a risk

00:09:17.000 --> 00:09:19.840
to have a premature heart
attack at a young age,

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:21.387
maybe it's worth being on that medication.

00:09:21.387 --> 00:09:23.417
(speaking in foreign language)

00:09:23.417 --> 00:09:26.084
(ominous music)

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:44.370
Smoking is bad for you.

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:47.450
I don't feel like I need to
say that but at the same time,

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:49.330
I'm gonna because I want you to know

00:09:49.330 --> 00:09:52.310
that smoking increases
risk of not only cancers,

00:09:52.310 --> 00:09:54.230
not just lung cancer, all cancers,

00:09:54.230 --> 00:09:55.970
like so many cancers throughout your body,

00:09:55.970 --> 00:09:58.090
other lung diseases like COPD,

00:09:58.090 --> 00:09:59.960
chronic bronchitis, emphysema.

00:09:59.960 --> 00:10:01.950
increases in heart disease, strokes.

00:10:01.950 --> 00:10:03.650
Just by smoking,

00:10:03.650 --> 00:10:06.810
we lose approximately half a million lives

00:10:06.810 --> 00:10:10.170
due to smoking in the
United States every year.

00:10:10.170 --> 00:10:11.340
We in the medical community know

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:14.830
that cigarettes contain
over 600 ingredients

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:16.690
and when you light them
on fire and burn them,

00:10:16.690 --> 00:10:18.860
that creates over 7,000 chemicals

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:20.180
that you're inhaling into your body.

00:10:20.180 --> 00:10:22.780
And while nicotine may make
you feel good temporarily,

00:10:22.780 --> 00:10:25.300
it goes away and it's
highly, highly addictive.

00:10:25.300 --> 00:10:27.050
It's one of the most
addictive things out there.

00:10:27.050 --> 00:10:27.913
That's why it's so dangerous.

00:10:27.913 --> 00:10:30.196
(speaking in foreign language)

00:10:30.196 --> 00:10:32.863
(ominous music)

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:40.060
I hope this episode helps
you quit smoking if you do.

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:41.540
If you're not gonna do it for yourself,

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:43.170
do it for these little red blood cells.

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:45.110
They don't deserve the stress
that we're putting on them.

00:10:45.110 --> 00:10:47.210
Quit smoking and if you need
help, talk to your doctor

00:10:47.210 --> 00:10:49.860
'cause we actually have so many
options of helping you quit.

00:10:49.860 --> 00:10:51.810
Not only from a behavioral
therapy perspective,

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:53.390
we have some medications that can help,

00:10:53.390 --> 00:10:54.880
group counseling, et cetera.

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:55.760
Talk to your doctor.

00:10:55.760 --> 00:10:56.950
If you have that motivation,

00:10:56.950 --> 00:10:59.220
share it with us and we'll
be happy to help you out.

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:00.739
This is a great medical show.

